.ly_state/session\phase3_9_summary.md:23:New function `fold_level_dsr()` in `packages/quant/src/omega/quant/validation/dsr.py`. Treats each fold's per-bar Sharpe as one IID observation:
.ly_state/session\phase3_9_summary.md:25:- Skew/kurt computed over fold Sharpes, not over daily returns
.ly_state/session\phase3_9_summary.md:43:- **fold_SR_ann ≈ pool_SR_ann** at 1B/10B (within ~0.7%); diverges to ~54% at 100B because pooled SR is `mean(pooled_returns)/std(pooled_returns)` while fold SR is `mean_of(fold_SR)`, and pooled std differs from per-fold std when fold-level variance is heterogeneous (heavy-slippage tail dominates at 100B). Both are valid Sharpe estimators; they only coincide when fold volatilities are identical.
.ly_state/session\phase3_9_summary.md:45:- **fold_DSR < 0.95 across all 3 capitals** — confirms the strategy is real but not statistically deflation-proof at this fold count. To pass DSR=0.95 we'd need either more independent folds (longer history) or much tighter fold-Sharpe variance.
.ly_state/session\phase3_9_summary.md:53:3. `test_fold_level_dsr_does_not_inflate_with_oversampled_pooled_bars` — fold-level only sees fold Sharpes, not raw bars.
.ly_state/session\phase3_9_summary.md:58:8. `test_fold_level_dsr_zero_variance` — degenerate inputs (all identical fold Sharpes) handled.
